    <h2>Pro Tips to Set Your Rates & Avoid Undercharging</h2>
    <p>
      To build a profitable, sustainable freelance business, implement these proven pricing principles:
    </p>

    <div class="step-guide">
      <div class="step-item">
        <h3>1. Calculate Your Real Billable Hours</h3>
        <p>
          A full-time freelancer does not work 40 billable hours per week. Roughly 30% to 40% of your working hours are spent on non-billable tasks (pitching clients, sending invoices, administrative emails, and taxes). Base your target hourly calculations on 20 to 25 billable hours per week.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="step-item">
        <h3>2. Build in a 30% Tax and Overhead Buffer</h3>
        <p>
          As an independent contractor, you are responsible for self-employment taxes, healthcare, paid time off, and business subscriptions. Always add a minimum 30% margin on top of your personal living wage target.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="step-item">
        <h3>3. Always Present Tiered Proposals (Good / Better / Best)</h3>
        <p>
          When submitting proposals, present three structured tiers (e.g., <em>Tier 1: 1 Video ($200)</em> | <em>Tier 2: 3 Videos + 5 Photos ($550)</em> | <em>Tier 3: 5 Videos + 15 Photos + 90-Day Ad Rights ($1,100)</em>). Tiering anchors your value and empowers clients to select higher-value packages.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="step-item">
        <h3>4. Charge Separately for Raw Footage and Project Files</h3>
        <p>
          Never include unedited B-roll or raw project files (.PRPROJ, .FCPX) in your standard rate. If a brand wants raw footage ownership to repurpose future ads, charge a 50% to 100% buyout fee.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>Common Mistakes Creators Make While Pricing Services</h2>
    <p>
      Avoid these costly pricing errors that drain freelance profitability:
    </p>

    <div class="callout-box warning">
      <div class="callout-title">
        <span>⚠️</span> Critical Freelance Pitfalls
      </div>
      <p style="margin: 0; font-size: 0.95rem;">
        Avoid these common operational mistakes to safeguard your margins and prevent creative burnout.
      </p>
    </div>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pricing Exclusively on Filming Time:</strong> Forgetting to account for hours spent scripting hooks, storyboarding, prop sourcing, color grading, and client communication.</li>
      <li><strong>Granting Perpetual Paid Ad Rights for Free:</strong> Allowing brands to use your face and video in commercial paid ads forever without ongoing licensing compensation.</li>
      <li><strong>Allowing Unlimited Revision Rounds:</strong> Failing to specify revision limits in contracts, leading to endless feedback loops and doubled project hours.</li>
      <li><strong>Starting Work Without an Upfront Deposit:</strong> Delivering finished assets before collecting a 50% upfront payment, exposing yourself to non-payment risk.</li>
      <li><strong>Discounting Rates Without Reducing Scope:</strong> Lowering your quote when a client pushes back without removing deliverables, which devalues your baseline rates.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Best Practices for Presenting & Negotiating Rates with Brands</h2>
    <p>
      Follow these professional standards when communicating with brands:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Use Clean Itemized Proposals:</strong> Send professional PDF quotes outlining exactly what is included: asset count, video aspect ratios (9:16 vs 16:9), resolution, revision limits, and usage licensing terms.</li>
      <li><strong>Negotiate Deliverables Instead of Price:</strong> If a client's budget is lower than your quote, reduce the deliverables to match their budget (e.g., <em>'I can accommodate your $500 budget by providing 2 videos instead of 3.'</em>).</li>
      <li><strong>Require Signed Agreements Before Production:</strong> Always use a written Scope of Work (SOW) agreement that clearly defines deadlines, payment milestones, cancellation fees, and usage rights expiration dates.</li>
    </ol>
  </div>
